## 4. Workload model
Open model (arrival-rate executors): new iterations start at the planned rate whether or not the system slows down. A closed model would hide saturation through coordinated omission. Sizing uses Little's Law: **N = X × (R + Z)**.

| ID | Users at peak | Iteration time (s) (R+Z) | Arrival rate (iter/min) | Data file |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
{% for w in workload.scenarios %}
| {{ w.id }} | {{ w.users }} | {{ w.iteration_seconds }} | {{ w.rate_per_min if w.rate_per_min is not none else "constant browser VUs" }} | {{ w.data_file or "-" }} |
{% endfor %}

Assumptions: server time {{ workload.nfr.est_response_s }}s per request until a baseline exists; think time is randomised ±30%. **Replace these estimates with production analytics or access-log data where available**, then re-run `design`.
Hybrid model: protocol-level VUs generate the load, and {{ workload.nfr.browser_vus }} real-browser VU(s) measure what end users experience (Core Web Vitals).

